345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 103 0 R
/T (Translating a schematic symbol into physical hardware requires associating each logical component with an IPC-7351 compliant PCB footprint. In eSim / KiCad, this association is performed using the Cvpcb Footprint Associator tool. Choosing appropriate footprints is one of the most critical steps in physical hardware design: pad geometries, solder mask clearances, lead pitch, pin numbering, and courtyard boundaries must match the physical parts perfectly to guarantee mechanical assembly and soldering yield.)
/E (Translating a schematic symbol into physical hardware requires associating each logical component with an IPC-7351 compliant PCB footprint. In eSim / KiCad, this association is performed using the Cvpcb Footprint Associator tool. Choosing appropriate footprints is one of the most critical steps in physical hardware design: pad geometries, solder mask clearances, lead pitch, pin numbering, and courtyard boundaries must match the physical parts perfectly to guarantee mechanical assembly and soldering yield.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 103 0 R
/T (In KiCad Cvpcb, footprints are assigned using a three-pane interactive filtering interface. The left pane lists available footprint libraries, the center pane lists the 22 schematic component symbols, and the right pane filters available footprints by pin count, library name, and keyword search. Every assignment was cross-checked against manufacturer component datasheets \(Texas Instruments for TPS63001, Espressif for ESP32-WROOM-32, InvenSense for MPU-6050, and standard IPC-7351 specifications for discrete SMD passives\) to ensure absolute dimensional compatibility before netlist synchronization.)
/E (In KiCad Cvpcb, footprints are assigned using a three-pane interactive filtering interface. The left pane lists available footprint libraries, the center pane lists the 22 schematic component symbols, and the right pane filters available footprints by pin count, library name, and keyword search. Every assignment was cross-checked against manufacturer component datasheets \(Texas Instruments for TPS63001, Espressif for ESP32-WROOM-32, InvenSense for MPU-6050, and standard IPC-7351 specifications for discrete SMD passives\) to ensure absolute dimensional compatibility before netlist synchronization.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
